<BaseLayout
  title="Guide | Eloqium TTS"
  description="A detailed user guide for installing and configuring Eloqium TTS on Android, including voices, speech controls, punctuation, text processing, dictionaries, language, and reset settings."
  currentPath="/guide/"
>
  <div class="shell"><PageIntro eyebrow="Guide" title="Set up Eloqium and learn what its controls do." description="The guide follows the names used in the Android application. Start with installation if you are new to Eloqium, or use the section links to find a particular setting." /></div>

  <nav class="guide-map" aria-label="Guide sections"><div class="shell guide-map-inner"><a href="#install"><span>01</span>Install and enable</a><a href="#choose-a-voice"><span>02</span>Choose a voice</a><a href="#speech-controls"><span>03</span>Speech controls</a><a href="#text-processing"><span>04</span>Text processing</a><a href="#dictionary-settings"><span>05</span>User Dictionary</a><a href="#language-audio"><span>06</span>Language and audio</a><a href="#advanced"><span>07</span>Advanced</a><a href="#reset"><span>08</span>Reset and troubleshooting</a></div></nav>

  <section class="page-section guide-section" id="install" aria-labelledby="install-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">01 · Installation</p><h2 id="install-heading">Install the app and make Eloqium the preferred TTS engine.</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>Android’s engine selection and Eloqium’s own settings are separate parts of the initial setup.</p></div></div><ol class="steps"><li><span>01</span><div><h3>Download and install the APK</h3><p>Download the current release APK from the Download page. Install the file on a supported Android device and open the Eloqium application.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Select Eloqium TTS</h3><p>On the Eloqium home screen, open <strong>System TTS Settings</strong>. In Android’s text-to-speech settings, select <strong>Eloqium TTS</strong> as the Preferred Engine.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><h3>Open Eloqium settings</h3><p>Return to Eloqium and open <strong>Eloqium settings</strong>. You can then select a language and voice profile and adjust any other controls you want to change.</p></div></li></ol></div></section>

  <section class="page-section alt guide-section" id="choose-a-voice" aria-labelledby="voice-guide-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">02 · Voice</p><h2 id="voice-guide-heading">Choose a regional language and voice profile.</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>The application separates language selection from the voice profile, allowing the same set of named presets to be used across the supported regional variants.</p></div></div><div class="task-grid"><article><h3>Choose a voice profile</h3><p>In <strong>Voice → Voice profile</strong>, choose one of the available voice presets. Your selection determines the voice profile used for synthesis within the selected regional language.</p></article><article><h3>Set the language</h3><p>Open <strong>Language &amp; Audio → Language</strong> and choose the regional language variant you want Eloqium to use. The <strong>Force language</strong> switch can be enabled when Eloqium should use that configured language rather than following another language request from the calling application.</p></article><article><h3>Change speech rate</h3><p>Use <strong>Speech rate</strong> to choose a relative rate from -10 to +10. Enable <strong>Force rate</strong> when your configured value should take precedence over the rate supplied by an application. <strong>Unlock maximum rate</strong> extends the upper range available to the forced rate setting.</p></article><article><h3>Change pitch</h3><p>Use <strong>Pitch</strong> for a relative pitch adjustment. Enable <strong>Force pitch</strong> when Eloqium should use your configured pitch rather than a pitch value supplied by the calling application.</p></article><article><h3>Change volume</h3><p>Enable <strong>Force volume</strong> to apply Eloqium’s relative digital gain setting to the synthesized audio. Android’s system playback volume remains responsible for the final output level.</p></article><article><h3>Adjust voice character</h3><p>Under <strong>Voice Characteristics</strong>, use <strong>Inflection</strong>, <strong>Head size</strong>, <strong>Roughness</strong>, and <strong>Breathiness</strong> when you want to change the character of the selected voice beyond ordinary rate and pitch adjustments.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="page-section guide-section" id="speech-controls" aria-labelledby="speech-guide-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">03 · Speech controls</p><h2 id="speech-guide-heading">Understand the Force switches before changing them.</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>A Force switch is useful when the application making the Android TTS request should not be allowed to replace the corresponding Eloqium setting.</p></div></div><div class="prose"><h3>Force rate</h3><p>When <strong>Force rate</strong> is off, Eloqium maps the speech-rate value supplied by the calling Android application. When it is on, Eloqium maps the value you selected under <strong>Speech rate</strong>. <strong>Unlock maximum rate</strong> determines whether the forced rate can use the extended upper range.</p><h3>Force pitch</h3><p>When <strong>Force pitch</strong> is off, the pitch requested by the calling application is used. When it is on, the configured <strong>Pitch</strong> value is used instead.</p><h3>Force volume</h3><p>When <strong>Force volume</strong> is off, Eloqium leaves the PCM buffers unmodified for digital gain. When it is on, the selected <strong>Volume</strong> value scales the synthesized 16-bit PCM samples before they are supplied to Android’s audio stream. Increasing this value can raise the digital signal level, but it does not bypass the device’s normal multimedia volume control.</p><h3>ECI Voice Tags</h3><p><strong>ECI Voice Tags</strong> determines whether compatible inline voice and control annotations using Eloqium’s supported backquote pattern are allowed through the text-processing path. Leave this enabled when an application deliberately sends those tags; otherwise the setting can remain at its default.</p><LinkArrow href="/voices/" label="Review the available voices" /></div></div></section>

  <section class="page-section alt guide-section" id="text-processing" aria-labelledby="text-guide-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">04 · Text Processing</p><h2 id="text-guide-heading">Configure the way written text is prepared for speech.</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>These controls are especially useful for screen-reader output, technical text, documents, and messages containing symbols or structured numbers.</p></div></div><div class="task-grid"><article><h3>Process punctuation</h3><p>Enable <strong>Process punctuation</strong> when you want selected punctuation marks to be spoken by name. Then choose <strong>Punctuation level</strong> from None, Some, Most, All, or Custom. None leaves punctuation in the ordinary phrasing path. Some speaks a core set of symbols. Most adds structural punctuation such as brackets, quotation marks, dashes, colons, and semicolons. All additionally speaks periods, commas, question marks, and exclamation marks. Custom lets you choose the characters to verbalize.</p></article><article><h3>Number processing</h3><p>Enable <strong>Use number processing</strong> when you want multi-digit sequences grouped before synthesis. <strong>Smart</strong> selects formatting based on the context of the number and supported patterns. <strong>Digits</strong> separates each digit, <strong>Pairs</strong> groups from the left in pairs, and <strong>Triplets</strong> groups from the right in threes.</p></article><article><h3>Capital indication</h3><p>Configure how uppercase letters are vocalized. <strong>None</strong> preserves standard speech. <strong>Pitch raise</strong> raises the pitch only for standalone uppercase one-letter words, such as <code>A</code> or <code>I</code>. <strong>Say capital</strong> speaks &ldquo;capital&rdquo; before the letter.</p></article><article><h3>Abbreviations</h3><p>Enable <strong>Use abbreviations</strong> to expand supported forms from Eloqium’s built-in abbreviation dictionary. This includes common titles, address abbreviations, and several frequently used textual abbreviations.</p></article><article><h3>Emoji and emoticons</h3><p><strong>Emoji / Emoticon</strong> enables spoken descriptions for supported emoji and a defined set of ASCII emoticons. The processor protects text patterns that are likely to be URLs, times, ratios, paths, or code expressions.</p></article><article><h3>Intonation pauses</h3><p><strong>Intonation pauses</strong> controls the pause-processing mode used around punctuation. With the setting enabled, the normal pause-preserving path is used. With it disabled, additional pause markers are inserted during chunk preparation.</p></article><article><h3>Phrase prediction</h3><p>Phrase prediction is enabled by default. The application can configure this behavior, and the enabled path adds the OpenEVV <code>pp1</code> control information during chunk preparation.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="page-section guide-section" id="dictionary-settings" aria-labelledby="dictionary-guide-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">05 · User Dictionary</p><h2 id="dictionary-guide-heading">Create pronunciation and replacement rules.</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>The User Dictionary system can contain several named dictionaries, each associated with a regional language.</p></div></div><div class="prose"><p>Under <strong>User Dictionary</strong>, make sure the dictionary system is enabled. Open <strong>Dictionaries</strong> to create and manage named dictionaries for the active regional language.</p><p>A <strong>TEXT</strong> entry replaces the matching source text with another text string. A <strong>PRONUNCIATION</strong> entry supplies a phonetic pronunciation instead. Each entry can use Exact match, Starts with, Ends with, or Contains matching and can be configured as case-sensitive or case-insensitive.</p><p>When several rules overlap, Eloqium uses deterministic precedence so that longer and more specific rules are considered before broader ones. The replacement pass is non-cascading, so a newly produced replacement does not become input for another dictionary rule in the same pass.</p><p>Use the Dictionary page for the complete matching descriptions and information about JSON and IBM <code>.dic</code> files.</p><LinkArrow href="/dictionary/" label="Open the complete Dictionary guide" /></div></div></section>

  <section class="page-section alt guide-section" id="language-audio" aria-labelledby="language-audio-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">06 · Language &amp; Audio</p><h2 id="language-audio-heading">Choose the language behavior and sampling rate.</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>Language controls affect which regional language Eloqium uses for synthesis requests; sampling rate controls the rate of the native audio output.</p></div></div><div class="prose"><h3>Force language</h3><p>Enable <strong>Force language</strong> when you want Eloqium to use the language selected under <strong>Language</strong> instead of following a different language request from the calling application.</p><h3>Sampling rate</h3><p>The <strong>Sampling rate</strong> setting provides seven supported rates: 8,000 Hz, 11,025 Hz, 16,000 Hz, 22,050 Hz, 32,000 Hz, 44,100 Hz, and 48,000 Hz. The default value is 11,025 Hz. This setting changes the sample rate used by the native synthesis path; it is not an Android device compatibility requirement.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="page-section guide-section" id="advanced" aria-labelledby="advanced-guide-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">07 · Advanced</p><h2 id="advanced-guide-heading">Eloqium foreground service</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>Foreground-service settings can improve reliability during background speech.</p></div></div><div class="prose"><h3>Background reliability</h3><p>The <strong>Eloqium foreground service</strong> section provides controls intended to improve speech reliability when Eloqium runs in the background, particularly on devices with restrictive background process management.</p><h3>App notifications</h3><p>Shows whether notifications are allowed and opens system notification settings. The foreground service uses an ongoing notification while active to indicate that the service is running.</p><h3>Show persistent notification</h3><p>When enabled, keeps a notification visible in the notification shade while the foreground service is active.</p><h3>Battery optimization</h3><p>Shows current battery optimization status (Unrestricted or Optimized) and provides a shortcut to Android's battery settings. Battery optimization behavior is controlled by Android and device manufacturers and may differ between devices.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="page-section alt guide-section" id="reset" aria-labelledby="reset-heading"><div class="shell guide-layout"><div><p class="section-kicker">08 · Reset</p><h2 id="reset-heading">Restore the application’s default settings.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Open the <strong>Reset</strong> category and choose <strong>Reset all settings to the default</strong>. Eloqium will restore the stored configuration values to their initial defaults.</p><p>If Android continues using a different TTS engine, return to <strong>System TTS Settings</strong> and confirm that <strong>Eloqium TTS</strong> is selected as the Preferred Engine. If an application requests a language or voice that you do not want to follow, review the relevant Force setting in Eloqium’s configuration.</p><LinkArrow href="/download/" label="Review installation requirements" /></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
